---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import ProseWithToc from '../components/ProseWithToc.astro'

const demoParg = `# demo.parg
grammar Demo version "1.0.0" {
  skip = trivia
  trivia = 1*" "

  digit = %x30-39
  number = 1*digit
  publisher = %i"iso" / %i"ieee"

  origin = publisher as publisher
  serial = number as number

  iso_identifier = origin serial
}

bindings iso_identifier {
  publisher -> publisher (string)
  number -> number (integer)
}

test identifier {
  accept "iso 12345"
  accept "IEEE 42"
  reject "iso"
  capture "ieee 42" { publisher: "ieee", number: "42" }
}

entry identifier: iso_identifier`

const demoTypeScript = `import init, { PargArtifactJs } from "@parsanol/wasm";

await init();

// Checksum verified before any parsing
const parg = new PargArtifactJs(artifactJson);

const bound = parg.parseAndBind("identifier", "iso 12345");
// => { publisher: "iso", number: 12345 }

// Recovery envelope: deepest position, partial tree, resync spans
const envelope = JSON.parse(parg.parseShapeRecovering("identifier", input));

// Generated binding-requirements types for your model
const ts = parg.schemaTypescript();`

const demoRuby = `require "parsanol"
require "json"

document = Parsanol::PARG::Parser.new(File.read("demo.parg")).parse
result   = Parsanol::PARG::Compiler.compile(document)
File.write("demo.artifact.json", JSON.generate(result.envelope))

artifact = Parsanol::PARG::Artifact.load("demo.artifact.json")
artifact.parse_and_bind("identifier", "iso 12345")
# => { publisher: "iso", number: 12345 }`

const lifecycle = [
  {
    step: '1',
    title: 'Write',
    body: 'One .parg text file: rules in an ABNF-flavored style, entries, bindings, in-file tests. The compiler lints it — shadowed alternatives and order-dependence are build failures, not runtime surprises.',
  },
  {
    step: '2',
    title: 'Compile',
    body: 'Parsanol::PARG::Compiler bakes the grammar into portable atoms, applies compile-time rewrites (skip injection, left-recursion tiers, resync policies), and runs every in-file test. A failing test fails the build.',
  },
  {
    step: '3',
    title: 'Artifact',
    body: 'A parsanol-tree/v2 envelope: the portable grammar JSON, bindings, embedded tests, the original source — and a sha256 checksum over the canonicalized content.',
  },
  {
    step: '4',
    title: 'Consume',
    body: 'Every runtime loads the same file, verifies the checksum, and parses identically: PargArtifact (Ruby), the parsanol-rs crate, PargArtifactJs (@parsanol/wasm), PargRuntime (TypeScript).',
  },
]

const features = [
  {
    title: 'Trivia, declared once',
    body: 'skip = trivia injects whitespace/comment consumption before every terminal; captured trivia rides with the next capture under comments:. atomic rules opt out for token-shaped bodies.',
    anchor: '/parg/language#skip',
  },
  {
    title: 'Recovery with owned spans',
    body: 'resync <rule> = <pattern> bakes a deterministic skip policy into the artifact: failures return the deepest position, the partial tree, and the exact spans consumed so parsing resumed.',
    anchor: '/parg/language#resync',
  },
  {
    title: 'Direct left recursion',
    body: 'expr = expr "*" expr / INT is rewritten at compile time into left-folding precedence tiers — alternatives in source order bind tightest-first — and every engine runs the identical fold.',
    anchor: '/parg/language#left-recursion',
  },
  {
    title: 'Lookbehind guards',
    body: '!<x and &<x inspect the text behind the position without consuming — the flank guards for intraword constraints like constrained emphasis.',
    anchor: '/parg/language#behind-guards',
  },
  {
    title: 'Tests travel with the grammar',
    body: 'accept / reject / capture / example / shape assertions run at compile time and ride the artifact, so every engine replays the same expectations — that is the conformance corpus.',
    anchor: '/parg/language#tests',
  },
  {
    title: 'Data, not code',
    body: 'alt from_table builds literal alternatives from a table at compile time; table_lookup preprocesses bound values; bindings map captures to typed model fields.',
    anchor: '/parg/language#bindings',
  },
  {
    title: 'Two tree editions',
    body: 'Edition v1 is the default capture tree. Edition v2 names every non-capturing rule reference, so consumers read a fully named tree — same grammar text apart from the header, different artifacts, checksums, and trees.',
    anchor: '/parg/language#editions',
  },
]
---
<Layout
  title="PARG — the grammar language — Parsanol"
  description="PARG is parsanol's grammar language: write once, compile to a checksummed artifact, parse identically on Ruby, Rust, WebAssembly, and TypeScript."
>
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
  <div class="flex gap-12">
    <aside class="w-64 flex-shrink-0 hidden lg:block">
      <div class="sticky top-20 text-sm space-y-1">
        <a href="/parg" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded bg-primary-100 dark:bg-primary-900/30 text-primary-700 dark:text-primary-300 font-medium">What is PARG?</a>
        <a href="/parg/language" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-gray-800">Language tour</a>
        <a href="/playground" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-gray-800">Playground</a>
        <a href="/errors" class="block px-3 py-1.5 rounded text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-gray-800">Diagnostics</a>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <ProseWithToc>
      <h1>PARG — the grammar language</h1>

      <p class="text-lg">
        PARG is how you write a parser for parsanol: one text file describing the grammar,
        compiled <strong>once</strong> into a checksummed artifact that Ruby, Rust,
        WebAssembly, and TypeScript all consume — building byte-identical trees and
        recovering from broken input identically.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose bg-primary-50 dark:bg-primary-900/20 border border-primary-100 dark:border-primary-900/40 rounded-xl p-5 my-8">
        <p class="text-primary-900 dark:text-primary-200 font-medium">
          Write once → compile → verify → parse everywhere. The grammar becomes data with a
          checksum, not code pinned to one language.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The lifecycle</h2>
      <div class="not-prose grid sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 my-6">
        {lifecycle.map((item) => (
          <div class="card">
            <div class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 font-bold text-sm mb-1">{item.step}</div>
            <div class="font-semibold mb-2">{item.title}</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Quick start</h2>
      <p>
        A grammar, its bindings, and an entry point — then compile it and parse. This is the
        whole workflow:
      </p>
      <div class="not-prose grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <div class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">demo.parg</div>
          <pre class="text-xs overflow-x-auto"><code>{demoParg}</code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <div class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">compile once, parse anywhere</div>
          <pre class="text-xs overflow-x-auto"><code>{demoRuby}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The same artifact parses the same input on the Rust side
        (<code>PargArtifact</code>) and in the browser (<code>PargArtifactJs</code> from
        <code>@parsanol/wasm</code>) — try the
        <a href="/playground">playground</a>, which runs the shipped wasm binary.
      </p>

      <h2>TypeScript, JavaScript, and the browser</h2>
      <p>
        The compiled artifact runs natively in TypeScript and JavaScript through
        <code>@parsanol/wasm</code> — the published wasm build this site's playground runs.
        Load it, and the checksum is verified before any parsing:
      </p>
      <div class="not-prose my-6 min-w-0">
        <div class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 mb-1">TypeScript / JavaScript</div>
        <pre class="text-xs overflow-x-auto"><code>{demoTypeScript}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <p>
        The artifact exposes its whole surface to JavaScript:
        <code>parseAndBind</code> for one-step parsing and binding,
        <code>parseShape</code> and <code>parseShapeRecovering</code> for the raw
        parsanol-shape tree and the recovery envelope (deepest position, partial tree,
        declared resync spans), <code>applyBindings</code> for two-step binding,
        <code>runTests</code> for the embedded suite, and <code>schemaTypescript()</code>,
        which generates the binding-requirements types for your model from the artifact.
        The <a href="/playground">playground</a> is itself a TypeScript consumer of the same
        binary — its recovery demo runs through <code>parseShapeRecovering</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>What the artifact guarantees</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Integrity</strong> — the sha256 checksum over the canonicalized envelope is verified on load; a mismatch fails loudly, never silently mis-parses.</li>
        <li><strong>Portability</strong> — the grammar section is the exact wire format every engine registers; one serialization, four runtimes.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-containment</strong> — the original .parg text rides inside, so a runtime can rebuild atoms without the source file.</li>
        <li><strong>Behavior pinned</strong> — the in-file tests travel with the artifact; consumers replay them (this site's playground badge does exactly that).</li>
        <li><strong>Versioned</strong> — capture renames are major, new alternatives minor, literal fixes patch; model-side-only changes move a separate <code>binding_version</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Editioned</strong> — the envelope declares <code>edition</code> (<code>1</code> or <code>2</code>); the Ruby and Rust loaders validate it — earlier quoted spellings still load — and reject an unknown edition rather than parsing it with the wrong tree shape.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the language has</h2>
      <div class="not-prose grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
        {features.map((feature) => (
          <a href={feature.anchor} class="card hover:border-primary-400 transition block">
            <div class="font-semibold mb-2">{feature.title}</div>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">{feature.body}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Runtime boundaries, stated plainly</h2>
      <p>
        Grammars using runtime state (<code>state</code>/<code>set</code>/<code>switch</code>
        atoms or <code>custom</code> Ruby classes) are Ruby-tier by contract: the Rust and
        wasm engines reject such artifacts loudly at the boundary instead of mis-parsing
        them. Everything else on this page runs on every engine — and the
        <a href="/feature-comparison">engine comparison</a> shows how that is enforced.
      </p>

      <h2>Normative documents</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/parsanol/parsanol-ruby/blob/main/docs/parg-language-guide.md">parg-language-guide.md</a> — the friendly edition (this page distills it).</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/parsanol/parsanol-ruby/blob/main/docs/parg-semantics.md">parg-semantics.md</a> — the normative match relation, flatten laws, trivia rewrite, and the corpus coverage matrix.</li>
        <li><a href="/parg/language">The language tour</a> — the syntax on one page, with examples.</li>
      </ul>
  </ProseWithToc>
  </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
